<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leipzig</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leipzig is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, that grew from a medieval market town into one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s great trade-fair centres. Its university, founded in 1409, and its book trade made it a centre of learning, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> worked there from 1723. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in October 1813 ended French power in Germany, and the city's Monday demonstrations of 1989 helped bring down the East German regime.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leipzig.jpg" alt="Leipzig" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Derbrauni · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blick_vom_City-Hochhaus_Leipzig_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Division of Leipzig of 1485 the brothers Ernest and Albert split the inheritance: Ernest kept the electorate with Wittenberg and most of Thuringia, while Albert received Meissen with Dresden and <b>Leipzig</b>, founding the Albertine line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Railways spread rapidly after the line from Nuremberg to Fürth opened in 1835 and the <b>Leipzig</b>–Dresden line followed in 1839, and the Rhineland, the Ruhr, Saxony and Silesia began to industrialise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Gerhard von Scharnhorst and August von Gneisenau rebuilt it with a general staff, promotion by merit and the Landwehr militia, and Prussian troops under Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher fought at <b>Leipzig</b> in 1813 and Waterloo in 1815.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1519, in a debate at <b>Leipzig</b> with the theologian Johann Eck, Luther declared that church councils as well as popes could err.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>A popular reading, apparently first widely published in a pamphlet by Philip Nicodemus Lebrecht in <b>Leipzig</b> in 1731, makes it an allegory of the nations that conquered the Land of Israel, from Assyria and Babylon to the Crusaders and the Ottomans, with the goat as the Jewish people…</blockquote><a href="/songs/chad-gadya">Chad Gadya</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody first appeared in print in <b>Leipzig</b> in 1782, in Lieder für Freunde der Geselligen Freude, with Kindleben's German words, but it was evidently well known before.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gaudeamus-igitur">Gaudeamus igitur</a></li></ul></section>
